{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And you shall eat old store long preserved.</b> After the nations diminish your farmers and vintners, the grain will multiply from year to year, so that what little remains of the nations will suffice to serve you in gathering produce. And apart from this, you will be sustained without distress, as it says: יְהִי פִסַּת בַּר בָּאָרֶץ בְּרֹאשׁ הָרִים יִרְעַשׁ כַּלְּבָנוֹן פִּרְיוֹ (Psalms 72:16), and as they said in the last chapter of Ketuvot: the land of Israel is destined to produce cakes of bread and fine garments." "<b>And you shall bring out the old from before the new.</b> You will have from the stored grain enough for your needs, even though from the time it becomes old you bring forth from it to sustain the remnant of the nations, as it says (Isaiah 60:12): כִּי הַגּוֹי וְהַמַּמְלָכָה אֲשֶׁר לֹא יַעַבְדוּךָ יֹאבֵדוּ. And the reason you bring forth a thing that sustains life from your land to the nations is that the new has already come, and you need not fear it will be lacking for you — as was the case, according to their dictum (Bava Batra 90a): אֵין מוֹצִיאִין מֵאֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל דָּבָר שֶׁיֵּשׁ בּוֹ חַיֵּי נֶפֶשׁ, כְּגוֹן יֵינוֹת שְׁמָנִים וּסְלָתוֹת."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The rabbinic citation is from b. Ketuvot 111b (end of the last chapter, שהשוכר את הפועל). Sforno's Hebrew preserves the Aramaic גְּלֻסְקָאוֹת וּכְלֵי מִלַּת in transliterated form.", 1 "The phrase כְּמוֹ שֶׁהָיָה ('as was the case') likely refers to the rabbinic restriction then in force, which will no longer apply."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:38:16.341038192Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}